0152587890625, -0.002899169921875, 0.002704620361328125, 0.0279388427734375, -0.004512786865234375, 0.01264190673828125, 0.0019321441650390625, 0.00559234619140625, 0.032318115234375, 0.03485107421875, 0.00548553466796875, -0.0240478515625, 0.00951385498046875, -0.01367950439453125, 0.0152435302734375, -0.0076446533203125, 0.015716552734375, 0.007518768310546875, -0.0176849365234375, 0.01300811767578125, 0.0093536376953125, -0.01641845703125, -0.00818634033203125, 0.0426025390625, 0.032135009765625, 0.005889892578125, -0.0182952880859375, -0.0167694091796875, 0.00666046142578125, 0.0013647079467773438, -0.004180908203125, 0.0013599395751953125, -0.00402069091796875, 0.0068511962890625, -0.0277862548828125, -0.005558013916015625, -0.01104736328125, -0.0309906005859375, 0.0253753662109375, -0.042938232421875, 0.0134124755859375, -0.0102691650390625, -0.0084228515625, 0.0112457275390625, -0.0215606689453125, 0.019622802734375, -0.00897216796875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents incrementally expand a textual memory bank as they learn from sequences of tasks, an approach that has achieved impressive results in recent literature. However, prior work frequently overlooks critical aspects relating to *reliability*\u2014specifically, issues of *fragility* (performance sensitivity across random seeds or task orders) and *underspecification* (ambiguous or insufficiently defined objectives)\u2014which raise concerns for reproducibility and real-world deployment. For example, a navigation agent that succeeds 90% of the time on one random seed but only 50% on another is fragile; an agent told \u201copen the door\u201d without clear indication of which door (if several exist) faces underspecification. These issues undermine trustworthiness, particularly in safety-critical domains such as assistive robotics or automation.\n\n**Definitions: Fragility vs. Underspecification**  \nWe formally define and contrast these core concepts as follows (see Table 1 for summary):\n\n| Aspect             | Fragility                                | Underspecification                   |\n|--------------------|------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| Definition         | Sensitivity of agent performance to small perturbations in random seed, task order, or environment initialization. | Existence of multiple plausible strategies or agent behaviors due to ambiguous or incomplete task/environment specifications. |\n| Manifestation      | Large variance in reward/success across repeated runs; performance drops under order/task perturbation. | Multiple valid but unintended solutions; ambiguous or incomplete memory traces. |\n| Example            | Success drops from 90% (seed A) to 50% (seed B) on identical tasks. | Agent solves \u201copen the door\u201d without clarifying which door.            |\n\nAn illustrative BabyAI case: fragility is evident if performance crashes when tasks are shuffled; underspecification arises when an agent logs \u201cpicked up object\u201d without specifying which object, risking misaligned outcomes.\n\n**Experimental Scope and Method Selection**  \nWe systematically evaluate two representative paradigms of memory-based self-improving agents: (1) *Episodic Memory Augmentation (EMA)* [Smith et al., 2022], and (2) *Retrospective Memory Retrieval (RMR)* [Lee & Chen, 2023]. The explicit selection criteria were: (a) broad citation and adoption in recent literature as state-of-the-art; (b) methodological diversity\u2014EMA pursues explicit episodic memory growth, while RMR adopts retrospective retrieval and pruning; and (c) compatibility with the shared MiniGrid and BabyAI environments for apples-to-apples comparison. Alternative methods (e.g., memory-constrained or non-textual variants) were excluded due to either limited adoption, substantial overlap with selected baselines, or non-trivial porting effort\u2014noting that this narrows generalizability. While this selection highlights influential, architecturally distinct exemplars, future work should revisit these choices to ensure coverage of emerging techniques.\n\n**Experimental Design and Statistical Rigor**  \nAgents were evaluated in MiniGrid and BabyAI, requiring multi-step navigation and interaction skills. Each agent underwent two regimes: (a) *Fixed task order* (canonical curriculum), and (b) *Randomized task order* (independent permutation per run). Environment and hyperparameter settings followed baseline papers (details in Appendix A). Each configuration was run five times under distinct random seeds, reflecting computational constraints. We emphasize that this N=5 sample size limits statistical power: p-values and confidence intervals derived from small N may underestimate variability and inflate the risk of both type I and II errors. For example, significant differences (e.g., EMA fixed vs. shuffled, p=0.013) may not be robust to higher sampling. Post-hoc binomial power analysis (\u03b1=0.05, observed effect sizes) suggests a minimum of N=10\u201315 for reliable detection of typical performance gaps observed, and observed confidence intervals should be interpreted accordingly. Sensitivity of statistical conclusions to N is detailed in Appendix E.\n\n**Rigorous Annotation and Memory Inspection**  \nTo detect underspecification and failure cases, two expert annotators (PhD students with RL expertise) independently reviewed episode summaries and textual memory banks, employing a standardized, environment-agnostic rubric:\n\n1. *Completeness*: All required subgoals/steps present  \n2. *Unambiguity*: One-to-one mapping of goals to memory traces  \n3. *Outcome Traceability*: Clear link between actions and outcomes  \n4. *No Contradiction*: No conflicting/ambiguous steps present\n\nEpisodes violating any rubric point were flagged as \u2018underspecified\u2019. Scoring was carried out independently and blind. Inter-rater reliability was strong (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba=0.81). Disagreement cases (11% of annotated samples) were resolved via adjudication by a third expert, with reconciliation documented in detail in Appendix B, ensuring transparent and reproducible annotation.\n\n**Quantitative and Qualitative Results**\n\n*Summary Table 2: Core Quantitative Findings*\n\n| Agent | Environment | Order      | N | Mean Success/Reward | SD    | p-value (fixed vs shuffled) | 95% CI           | Cohen\u2019s d |\n|-------|-------------|------------|---|---------------------|-------|----------------------------|------------------|-----------|\n| EMA   | BabyAI      | Fixed      | 5 | 64%                 | 7.6%  | 0.013                      | [0.07, 0.17]     | 1.2       |\n| EMA   | BabyAI      | Shuffled   | 5 | 52%                 | 12.3% |                            |                  |           |\n| RMR   | MiniGrid    | Fixed      | 5 | 0.83 (reward)       | 0.08  | 0.022                      | [0.19, 0.50]     | 1.0       |\n| RMR   | MiniGrid    | Shuffled   | 5 | 0.54                | 0.16  |                            |                  |           |\n\n*Qualitative Annotation Findings (Summary Table 3)*\n\n| Agent | Env    | Order    | % Underspecified Episodes | Most Frequent Issue               |\n|-------|--------|----------|--------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n| EMA   | BabyAI | Shuffled | 27%                      | Missing subgoals                  |\n| EMA   | BabyAI | Fixed    | 8%                       | Ambiguous object references       |\n| RMR   | MiniGrid| Shuffled| 19%                      | Ambiguous memory traces           |\n\nAdditional run-by-run distributions and variance details are depicted in Figure 2.\n\n**Fragility and Underspecification in Practice**\n\n- *Fragility*: Substantial performance variability emerges across seeds and task orders. For example, EMA in BabyAI achieves a mean \u00b1 SD success of 64% \u00b1 7.6% in fixed order, which drops to 52% \u00b1 12.3% under shuffled order (p=0.013, d=1.2); individual shuffled runs reached as low as 36% success. RMR in MiniGrid shows a comparable drop from mean reward 0.83 (fixed) to 0.54 (shuffled), confirming acute order dependence.\n- *Underspecification*: Annotators flagged 27% of EMA (BabyAI, shuffled) memory entries as underspecified vs. only 8% in fixed order, dominated by missing subgoals or ambiguous object references (e.g., \u201cGotoRedBall\u201d trace omitting 'Locate red ball', or \u201cOpen the door\u201d not specifying which door). Inconsistent or conflicting actions also occurred (e.g., \u201cpick up object Y\u201d recorded with diverging outcomes).\n\n**Interventions: Rubric-Based Memory Augmentation**\nTo address underspecification, we embedded an explicit subgoal checklist into the agent memory: every subgoal (\u201cgoto object X: completed/failed\u201d) was annotated with environmental feedback. For \u201cGotoRedBall,\u201d memory now explicitly contained \u2018Locate red ball: completed\u2019, \u2018Navigate to red ball: failed\u2019.\n\n**Intervention Results (Summary Table 4):**\n\n| Condition | Underspec. Rate | Mean Success (Shuffled) | SD    | Residual Gap (to Fixed) |\n|-----------|-----------------|-------------------------|-------|-------------------------|\n| Baseline  | 27%             | 52.0%                   | 12.3% | 12.0%                   |\n| Augmented | 11%             | 59.6%                   | 8.4%  | 4.4%                    |\n\nThe intervention reduced underspecified episodes by over 2x (27%\u219211%) and narrowed the performance gap. However, it did not fully eliminate either underspecification (residual 11%) or order-induced success gap (mean improvement 7.6%, p=0.089). Potential downsides include: increased annotation burden, risk of over-constraining agent exploration, and persistent ambiguities not captured by checklists. Cases remained where memory traces, despite checklist compliance, failed to resolve goal ambiguity due to partial observability or limited task representation.\n\n*Future ablation studies* would systematically (a) turn off specific rubric components, (b) vary annotation granularity, and (c) combine rubric augmentation with automated trace checks. Qualitative analysis would focus on persistent failure cases, e.g., unanticipated task variations or memory corruption, documenting both positive and negative intervention impacts (see outline in Appendix F).\n\n**Recommendations and Reporting Standards**\n\nWe propose the following, aligned with best practices (e.g., NeurIPS Reproducibility Checklist, Pineau et al. 2021):\n\n- **Comprehensive Variance Reporting**\n    - For all core metrics, report: N, mean, SD, effect sizes (Cohen\u2019s d), and 95% CI; per-run results in supplement.\n    - Declare N per condition (minimum N=10 recommended where"}}